# Development Lifecycle (Always On)

For non-trivial work in this project, follow this order and read the matching skill before acting.

## Phase map

| Phase | Use when | Skill / tool |
|-------|----------|--------------|
| 1. Planning | New feature, unclear requirements, greenfield | `/speckit-specify`, `/speckit-plan`, `/speckit-tasks`, `planning-and-task-breakdown`, `interview-me` |
| 2. Architecture | System design, APIs, module boundaries | `api-and-interface-design`, `documentation-and-adrs`, architecture skills under `~/.cursor/skills` |
| 3. Implementation | Building slices | `/speckit-implement`, `incremental-implementation`, `test-driven-development` |
| 4. Testing | Prove behavior, UI flows | `test-driven-development`, `browser-testing-with-devtools`, Playwright MCP |
| 5. Code review | Before merge / after substantive changes | `code-review-and-quality` |
| 6. Security | Auth, input, secrets, deps, hardening | `security-and-hardening` + `semgrep scan --config auto` |
| 7. Performance | Slow UI/API, Core Web Vitals | `performance-optimization` + `lighthouse <url>` |
| 8. Refactoring | Cleanup without behavior change | `code-simplification` + safe-refactoring rule |
| 9. Deployment | CI/CD, Docker, prod config | `ci-cd-and-automation`, `shipping-and-launch`, `observability-and-instrumentation` |

## Rules

1. Read `using-agent-skills` when unsure which skill applies.
2. Prefer Spec Kit skills (`speckit-*`) for plan → tasks → implement on new features.
3. Use Playwright MCP for real browser verification, not source-only guesses.
4. Run Semgrep after security-sensitive changes; Lighthouse after frontend perf work.
5. Do not skip review/security for auth, payments, data access, or public endpoints.
